  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 110 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThe flow aloft begins to amplify Wednesday morning, as the \nlarge upper high moves east towards the Ohio Valley, while an upper \ntrof with multiple large upper waves passing through it builds into \nthe central Rocky Mountain states. The first of several waves is \nforecast to pass well north of Iowa by all available guidance \nWednesday night into Thursday. A ridge building into the area in its \nwake will result in a dry forecast through Friday, with the main \nimpact being warmer temperatures and breezy conditions.\n\nWe will continue to see a stretch of southerly winds and warm \ntemperatures Thursday through Saturday, as the upper trof remains \ncentered over the Rockies. The NBM continues to show a good \npotential for mid to possibly upper 60s for each of these days! It \nwill also be breezy on Friday and Saturday with winds gusting to 20 \nto 25 MPH or higher Friday afternoon. With that said, there will be \nincreased cloud cover on Saturday as the front approaches, along \nwith rain chances in the 30 to 60% range. The QPF from this system \ncontinues to show potential for less than 0.25\" in all areas, as it \nis expected to be starved for moisture, with a ridge of high \npressure from the lower Appalachians through Oklahoma cutting off \nany Gulf moisture. \n\nFollowing the warm breezy days late this week will be a seasonal \ncold front arriving Saturday night or early Sunday, with highs \nlimited to the 40s behind it (which is far closer to normal than mid \nto upper 60s).",
